# Finiteness of the level-M modular function field over ℚ̄(j)

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.finiteDimensional_adjoin_coeffEmb_jq_of_neZero`
(`5a36e624-935b-5204-9d3e-4d7a975d19a0`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `09f1c3b1-7ff5-5a17-9b4d-f17ae83253d0`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $M$ be a nonzero natural number. Write $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ for `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and let
`coeffEmb` $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ denote the ring homomorphism $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q}) \to
\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}})$ obtained by applying the structure map $\mathbb{Q} \to
\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ to each coefficient of a Hahn series. The field `modularFunctionFieldBar M` is the
intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\bar{\mathbb{Q}})/\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated over
$\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ by the image under this coefficientwise embedding of `modularFunctionFieldFull
M`, the intermediate field of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\mathbb{Q})/\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the set
`divisorExpansions M`. Inside it sits the element determined by `coeffEmb` $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$
applied to `jq`, the Laurent series $q^{-1}\cdot(\text{ofPowerSeries of } \mathtt{jNumQ})$ over
$\mathbb{Q}$, i.e. the $q$-expansion of the modular invariant $j$; its membership in
`modularFunctionFieldBar M` comes from `jq_mem_full M` together with
`coeffEmb_mem_laurentBaseChange`. The assertion is that `modularFunctionFieldBar M` is
finite-dimensional as a vector space over the intermediate field $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}(j)$ obtained by
adjoining this single element to $\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ inside `modularFunctionFieldBar M`.

This is the statement that the modular function field of level $M$, after base change to
$\bar{\mathbb{Q}}$ and realised inside Laurent series in $q$, is a finite extension of the rational
function field generated by the $j$-expansion — the algebraic counterpart of the finiteness of the
covering $X(M) \to X(1)$ in the $j$-line. It is the general-level form of the prime-level finiteness
statement and underlies the construction of the function field of the modular curve used in the
Deligne–Rapoport model packages later in the development.

